The Security Council,
Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and the primary responsibility of the Security Council for the maintenance of international peace and security,
Recalling its relevant resolutions on non-proliferation, including resolution 1540 (2004) and resolution 2231 (2015), and the statement of its President S/PRST/2006/15,
Reaffirming the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons and the Treaty on Principles Governing the Activities of States in the Exploration and Use of Outer Space, including the Moon and Other Celestial Bodies,
Recalling the relevant General Assembly resolutions on the prevention of an arms race in outer space and on transparency and confidence-building measures in outer space activities,
Stressing that outer space is a global commons that should be explored and used exclusively for peaceful purposes and for the benefit and in the interests of all countries, irrespective of their degree of economic or scientific development,
Deeply concerned that the weaponization of outer space and an arms race in outer space would be a serious threat to international peace and security and would undermine strategic stability,
Reiterating that the prevention of the placement of nuclear weapons or any other weapons of mass destruction in outer space is an essential contribution to the maintenance of international peace and security and to the objectives of nuclear non-proliferation,
Emphasizing that all States parties to the Outer Space Treaty are bound by their obligations under article IV thereof, including not to place in orbit around the Earth any objects carrying nuclear weapons or any other kinds of weapons of mass destruction, install such weapons on celestial bodies, or station such weapons in outer space in any other manner,
Recognizing the important role of the Conference on Disarmament as the single multilateral disarmament negotiating forum, and of the Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space and the First Committee of the General Assembly in their respective mandates,
Emphasizing that the Security Council should respect the mandates and complementary roles of the relevant United Nations bodies and should not duplicate or replace their work on outer space security,
Welcoming continuing efforts by Member States to promote transparency and confidence-building measures in outer space activities and to prevent an arms race in outer space,
Noting the need for a comprehensive and cooperative approach to outer space security that takes into account the legitimate security interests of all States and preserves the right of all States to explore and use outer space for peaceful purposes,
1. Reaffirms the obligation of all States parties to the Outer Space Treaty to comply fully with article IV thereof, including not to place in orbit around the Earth any objects carrying nuclear weapons or any other kinds of weapons of mass destruction, install such weapons on celestial bodies, or station such weapons in outer space in any other manner;
2. Calls upon all Member States to refrain from any actions contrary to the objectives of the prevention of an arms race in outer space and to the peaceful use of outer space, and to fully respect their relevant international obligations;
3. Urges all States, in particular those with major space capabilities, to consider making political commitments not to be the first to place weapons in outer space and to refrain from actions that could trigger an arms race in outer space;
4. Encourages the Conference on Disarmament to intensify its efforts with a view to negotiating and adopting, on the basis of consensus, a legally binding instrument on the prevention of the placement of weapons in outer space, including nuclear weapons and other weapons of mass destruction, and on the prevention of an arms race in outer space;
5. Calls upon all States to promote transparency and confidence-building measures in outer space activities through bilateral, regional and multilateral channels, including within the relevant United Nations bodies;
6. Encourages international cooperation in the peaceful exploration and use of outer space, including capacity-building and the transfer of technology to developing countries on mutually agreed terms, in accordance with international law;
7. Reaffirms that the consideration by the Security Council of outer space security issues should be consistent with the respective mandates of the Conference on Disarmament, the Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space, the First Committee of the General Assembly and other relevant United Nations bodies, and should not duplicate or replace their work or pre-empt the outcome of their deliberations;
8. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and the primary responsibility of the Security Council for the maintenance of international peace and security,
Recalling its relevant resolutions on non-proliferation, including resolution 1540 (2004) and resolution 2231 (2015), and the statement of its President S/PRST/2006/15,
Reaffirming the Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons and the Treaty on Principles Governing the Activities of States in the Exploration and Use of Outer Space, including the Moon and Other Celestial Bodies,
Recalling the relevant General Assembly resolutions on the prevention of an arms race in outer space and on transparency and confidence-building measures in outer space activities,
Stressing that outer space is a global commons that should be explored and used exclusively for peaceful purposes and for the benefit and in the interests of all countries, irrespective of their degree of economic or scientific development, and recalling that outer space is the common heritage of humankind,
Deeply concerned that the weaponization of outer space and an arms race in outer space would be a serious threat to international peace and security and would undermine strategic stability,
Stressing that any placement of nuclear weapons or other weapons of mass destruction in outer space would violate article IV of the Outer Space Treaty, undermine strategic stability, and pose a grave threat to international peace and security, and emphasizing the importance of full compliance and transparency by all States party to the Treaty,
Reiterating that the prevention of the placement of nuclear weapons or any other weapons of mass destruction in outer space is an essential contribution to the maintenance of international peace and security and to the objectives of nuclear non-proliferation,
Emphasizing that all States parties to the Outer Space Treaty are bound by their obligations under article IV thereof, including not to place in orbit around the Earth any objects carrying nuclear weapons or any other kinds of weapons of mass destruction, install such weapons on celestial bodies, or station such weapons in outer space in any other manner,
Noting proposals for political commitments not to be the first to place weapons in outer space, and recognizing that such commitments could complement a legally binding instrument,
Recognizing the need for further efforts to address the prevention of the placement of all weapons in outer space, including conventional weapons, through appropriate multilateral forums, including the Conference on Disarmament,
Recognizing the important role of the Conference on Disarmament as the single multilateral disarmament negotiating forum, and of the Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space and the First Committee of the General Assembly in their respective mandates,
Emphasizing that the Security Council should respect the mandates and complementary roles of the relevant United Nations bodies and should not duplicate or replace their work on outer space security,
Welcoming continuing efforts by Member States to promote transparency and confidence-building measures in outer space activities and to prevent an arms race in outer space,
Noting the need for a comprehensive and cooperative approach to outer space security that takes into account the legitimate security interests of all States and preserves the right of all States to explore and use outer space for peaceful purposes,
1. Reaffirms the obligation of all States parties to the Outer Space Treaty to comply fully with article IV thereof, and calls upon all States, in particular those with major space capabilities, to refrain from developing, testing, producing, deploying or using nuclear weapons or any other weapons of mass destruction in outer space, and to comply fully with their relevant international obligations;
2. Calls upon all Member States to refrain from any actions contrary to the objectives of the prevention of an arms race in outer space and to the peaceful use of outer space, to fully respect their relevant international obligations, and to engage in dialogue to clarify any concerns regarding their outer space activities;
3. Encourages the Conference on Disarmament to continue its consensus-based consideration, as soon as possible, of all proposals relating to the prevention of an arms race in outer space, including the Russian-Chinese draft Treaty on the Prevention of the Placement of Weapons in Outer Space, the Threat or Use of Force against Outer Space Objects (PPWT), and proposals for a verifiable legally binding instrument that addresses the prevention of the placement of all weapons in outer space, including conventional weapons, nuclear weapons and other weapons of mass destruction, all existing and emerging threats, including anti-satellite weapons, and includes effective verification mechanisms, without prejudging the outcome of its deliberations or any future negotiating mandate;
4. Calls upon all States, in particular those with major space capabilities and nuclear-weapon States, to consider making political commitments not to be the first to place any weapons in outer space, including nuclear weapons and other weapons of mass destruction, pending the negotiation of a legally binding instrument, and stresses that such commitments should complement, not substitute for, a legally binding instrument;
5. Calls upon all States to promote transparency and confidence-building measures in outer space activities through bilateral, regional and multilateral channels, including within the relevant United Nations bodies;
6. Encourages international cooperation in the peaceful exploration and use of outer space, including capacity-building and the transfer of technology to developing countries, in particular least developed countries and small island developing States, on mutually agreed terms, consistent with international law, including non-proliferation obligations and national export controls, and respecting intellectual property regimes;
7. Reaffirms the primary responsibility of the Security Council for the maintenance of international peace and security and its readiness to address threats arising from outer space, while recognizing the complementary roles of the Conference on Disarmament, the Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space and the First Committee of the General Assembly, and reaffirms that the Council’s consideration of outer space security issues should be inclusive, transparent and non-politicized, and should not duplicate or replace their work or prejudge the outcome of their deliberations;
8. Calls upon all States to refrain from destructive anti-satellite tests, including direct-ascent missile tests, that create long-lived debris and pose a threat to the peaceful use of outer space, and to continue discussions on norms, rules and principles of responsible behaviour in outer space;
9. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

